course: myths
lesson: 7
title: Pets and farm animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Poppy hugs her new bunny and announces: "Snowy's getting a carrot every single day, because rabbits LOVE carrots!" She has watched Bugs Bunny crunching them for years, so she is sure.
explain: Pet myths come from cartoons, old farm pictures and kind people doing what their grandparents did: a saucer of milk for the cat, a saucer of milk for the hedgehog, a goldfish in a tiny round bowl.
explain: The trouble is that a kind idea with a wrong fact can make an animal ill: milk upsets most grown-up cats and makes hedgehogs sick, and too many carrots are like sweets for rabbits.
explain: Other myths are just funny, like the cow that lies down because rain is coming, or the bull that "hates red", when really bulls hardly tell red from green and charge at the cape because it moves.
explain: The best friends of animals check with a vet or an animal charity, and gently pass the real facts on without making anybody feel silly.
fact: Rabbits should eat mostly hay and grass with some leafy greens; carrots are sugary treats for now and then.
fact: Bulls are red-green colour-blind; it is the movement of a matador's cape, not its colour, that makes a bull charge.
fact: Dogs see colours, but fewer than we do, mostly blues and yellows; red and green look alike to them.
fact: Dogs age fast in their first year or two and slower after that, and big dogs age faster than small ones, so "seven dog years to one" is too simple.
fact: Cow's milk makes hedgehogs ill; give them water and meaty cat or dog food instead.
fact: Most grown-up cats cannot digest the sugar in milk well and get tummy upsets; water is the best drink for cats.
fact: Goldfish kept in small bowls stay stunted (small and badly grown) and stressed; in a big pond they can grow longer than 30 centimetres.
fact: Cows lie down for many reasons, such as resting and chewing their cud (bringing swallowed grass back up to chew again); lying down does not predict rain.
fact: Pigs can't sweat much, so they roll in mud to cool off and protect their skin from the sun; given space, they keep their toilet area away from where they sleep.
fact: A mother hamster may eat her babies when she is stressed, disturbed or short of food; it is instinct, not evil, so a new litter should be left in peace.
fact: Animals don't choose good or evil the way people do; their behaviour has reasons like food, safety and temperature.
exercise: Poppy wants to give her rabbit a carrot every day. What should a rabbit mostly eat? | Hay and grass, with some leafy greens | Carrots, because that's what cartoons always show | Crunchy lettuce leaves and nothing else at all | Bread and milk, just like baby birds are given
exercise: Why are carrots only a now-and-then treat for rabbits? | They are sugary, like sweets for bunnies | They are poisonous to rabbits | They make rabbits' long ears droop down over time | Rabbits can't chew anything orange
exercise: Grandad says bulls go mad at red. What really makes a bull charge at a cape? | The cape moving | The colour red | The smell of the cape | The noise of the crowd
exercise: Harvey says dogs see only black and white. What's true? | They see some colours, mostly blues and yellows | They see exactly the same colours as we do, and more | They see only red and green | They see no colours and no shapes
exercise: Auntie says one dog year equals seven human years. What's closer to the truth? | Dogs age fast at first, then slower, and big dogs age faster | Every dog year is exactly seven of ours | Small dogs age faster than big dogs | Dogs age at the same speed as people
exercise: Mrs Green puts out a saucer of milk for the hedgehog. What should she give instead? | Water and meaty cat or dog food | A warm saucer of milk with some honey | Bread soaked in a little cow's milk | Nothing, because hedgehogs never drink
exercise: Lucas's granny gives the kitten milk every day. What's the problem? | Most cats can't digest milk well and get tummy upsets | Milk makes cats' fur fall out | Cats only like milk from goats | There is no problem, because milk is the best drink for a cat
exercise: Ella says goldfish only grow to the size of their bowl. What really happens in a tiny bowl? | The fish is stunted and stressed | The fish stays small but perfectly healthy | The bowl stretches as the fish grows | The fish grows faster to fill it
exercise: How big can goldfish grow in a large pond? | Longer than 30 centimetres | About the size of a fingernail | Exactly the size of a bowl | As big as a dolphin
exercise: A farmer jokes a cow lying down means rain. Why do cows really lie down? | To rest and chew their cud, among other reasons | Because their knees can feel the rain coming on | Because they're scared of the thunder that follows | Only when they're about to be taken in to be milked
exercise: Josh says pigs are filthy and love dirt. Why do pigs roll in mud? | They can't sweat much, so mud cools them and protects their skin | They enjoy being smelly to scare enemies | Mud is their main food | They can't see clean ground
exercise: Given enough space, how do pigs keep their pen? | They keep the toilet area apart from their bed | They sleep right in their own mess and don't mind it | They never go to the toilet in a pen | They clean the pen with their tails
exercise: Sophie says hamsters eat their babies because they're evil. What's the real reason it can happen? | Stress, disturbance or lack of food make the mother act on instinct | Hamsters hate babies once they're born | Hamster mothers are always hungry for meat | It happens only to hamsters that bite people
exercise: What's the best way to help a hamster with a new litter? | Leave them in peace and keep things quiet | Pick up each of the babies to check it every day | Move the nest to a brighter, warmer place for them | Show the new babies to all your friends at once
exercise: Where did the idea that rabbits live on carrots mostly come from? | Cartoons like Bugs Bunny | Rabbit experts and vets | Farmers who grow carrots for rabbits | Wild rabbits stealing carrots at night
exercise: What can you do if you're not sure what a pet should eat? | Ask a vet or check an animal charity guide | Copy what a cartoon animal eats | Give it whatever you like to eat | Try lots of foods and see which it survives
test: "my nan always put milk out for the hedgehogs, they love it lol" How do you tell her kindly? | "That's so kind! The hedgehog charity says water and cat food are better, milk upsets them." | "You've been poisoning hedgehogs for years, Nan!" | "Hedgehogs need milk every night, keep going." | Say nothing, it doesn't matter
test: In a film, a bullfighter swaps the red cape for a blue one and the bull stands still, bored. What's wrong? | The bull would still charge, because it reacts to movement | Nothing would happen, because bulls only ever hate the colour red | The bull would charge harder at blue | Bulls run away from blue
test: You want to buy your dog Max a ball that's easy to spot on green grass. Which colour is best for him? | Blue | Red | Green | Orange
test: Your friend's Great Dane and your Chihuahua are both eight. Which is probably "older" in body terms? | The Great Dane, big dogs age faster | The Chihuahua, small dogs age faster | They're exactly the same age inside | Neither, dogs don't age after five
test: "Won a goldfish at the fair, gonna keep it in this little round bowl forever, it'll stay cute and tiny." What's wrong? | A tiny bowl stunts and stresses it; it needs a proper tank | Nothing, goldfish never grow anyway | It will keep on growing until it is too big and bursts the bowl | Goldfish prefer tiny bowls to ponds
test: How could you test the farmer's "cows lying down means rain" saying? | Keep a chart of cows lying down and rain for a few weeks | Ask the cows by mooing at them | Watch one cow in the field for one minute and write it down | Check whether cows like umbrellas
test: Josh wrinkles his nose: "Pigs are gross." What's a wise answer? | "They roll in mud to stay cool; they're actually tidy in their beds." | "You're right, pigs love being filthy." | "Pigs are gross because they eat mud." | "Only pink pigs are dirty."
test: A cartoon cat laps a big saucer of milk and purrs. Why shouldn't you copy it? | Most cats get tummy upsets from milk; water is best | Cartoon cats are a different species | Real cats need milk only at night, when they get thirsty | Milk is fine, but only cold milk
test: Sophie is upset and says her hamster is "a monster" for eating babies. What helps her understand? | It's sad, but it's instinct when a mother is scared or hungry | Her hamster is simply evil and should be punished for what it did | All hamster mothers do this every single time they have babies | She should have given all the babies away on the day they were born
test: Why do people keep believing pet myths even when they love their animals? | Old habits and pictures make the wrong idea look normal | Animals tell them the myths are true | Vets agree with all the old sayings | Pets like the wrong food better
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet appropriate to a domestic rabbit? | It consists principally of hay and grass, supplemented by leafy greens | It consists principally of carrots administered daily | It consists exclusively of bread and milk | It consists principally of fruit and cereals
exam: Identify the principal stimulus by which a bull is provoked to charge at a matador's cape. | The movement of the cape | The red colour of the cape | The scent of the material | The applause of the spectators
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the provision of cow's milk to hedgehogs. | It is harmful to them; water and meat-based pet food are to be preferred | It is the most suitable nourishment for wild hedgehogs, especially when warmed | It is harmful only when administered warm | It is beneficial if provided in small quantities daily
exam: It is given that a pig rolls in mud on a warm day. Ascertain the principal reason for this conduct. | It is unable to sweat sufficiently and is thereby cooled and protected | It derives pleasure from uncleanliness and seeks to repel its enemies by smell | It is seeking food buried within the mud | It is concealing itself from the farmer
